2.3 Employment

Average annual employment in Laramie County has increased by about 4,600 jobs over the ten‐year
period from 2005 to 2015. The largest jobs increase was in the Transportation and Warehousing
Sector, followed by Health Care and Social Assistance. However, in percentage terms, the Mining
Sector, which includes oil and gas extraction, saw the largest compound annual growth of almost
20% during this time. The drop in oil prices that began in the summer of 2014 undoubtedly has had an
impact on recent employment in this sector.

Table 5
Laramie County Annual Average Employment by Industry, 2005‐2015

2.4 Commute Patterns

Most of those employed in Laramie County and Cheyenne also live within the county and city,
respectively. However, the general trend over time, as seen in Figure 6, is that the percentage of
those employed in Laramie County but living outside of the county has increased over time. The
same is true for the percentage of jobs in Cheyenne filled by those who aren’t residents of the city.
The most recent figures available are from 2013, which show that about 23% of those employed in
Laramie County don’t live in the county. And, almost 46% of those employed in Cheyenne don’t live in
the city.

